├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ merge_pre_rz_into_ry_reduce_params                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 5 unique parameters per block (ry_shared, rz_pre_shared,          │
│                          │ zz_shared, rz_post_even, rz_post_odd), giving 32 total trainable parameters. Since RY     │
│                          │ followed immediately by RZ on the same qubit before any entangling gate is already a      │
│                          │ general single-qubit rotation subspace, the pre-entangling RZ layer is largely redundant  │
│                          │ with the RY layer for the purposes of generating the correlators used in readout (Z-Z     │
│                          │ expectations are insensitive to a global Z-rotation applied before any X/Y-mixing occurs, │
│                          │ since RZ commutes with the state's Z-diagonal component contributions in a limited way,   │
│                          │ but more importantly the RY layer already provides the necessary single-qubit             │
│                          │ expressivity to set up entanglement via ZZ gates). Removing the redundant "rz_pre_shared" │
│                          │ layer reduces parameters per block from 5 to 4 (24 total including gain/bias), improving  │
│                          │ parameter economy while the post-entangling even/odd RZ split retains the asymmetry       │
│                          │ needed for good class separation. This directly targets the task's strong reward for      │
│                          │ parameter economy without sacrificing the achieved 100% accuracy, since the removed layer │
│                          │ is applied uniformly to all qubits right after StatePrep/previous block and mostly acts   │
│                          │ as a global phase-like operation that the readout (Z-Z correlators) is largely            │
│                          │ insensitive to.                                                                           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0610                                                                                   │
