                        Patch Metadata - Gen 19/100 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ merge_zz_shared_ring                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses two separate ZZ parameters (zz_even, zz_odd) for the two halves   │
│                          │ of the entangling ring, giving 4 unique parameters per block (24 total circuit params).   │
│                          │ The first seed variant, which used a single shared ZZ parameter for the full ring (all    │
│                          │ edges sharing one angle) plus CZ fixed couplers, achieved the same perfect accuracy with  │
│                          │ substantially fewer parameters (20 total) and better parameter efficiency (0.05 vs 0.04)  │
│                          │ and faster convergence. This suggests the extra zz_even/zz_odd split does not add useful  │
│                          │ expressivity for this task. I merge zz_even and zz_odd into a single shared "zz_ring"     │
│                          │ parameter, cutting circuit parameters per block from 4 to 3 (18 total, plus 2 readout =   │
│                          │ 20), which should improve parameter economy and convergence speed while preserving the    │
│                          │ ring connectivity and full entangling power (since the two-qubit gate itself still varies │
│                          │ angle per training step, just tied together).                                             │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0631                                                                                   │
